<div class="main-text-content">
<h3>
 Sources and Certification Table - Church of San Carlo
</h3>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;">
 <thead>
  <tr style="background-color: #edf2f7;">
   <th style="width: 60%;">
    Statement / Historical Detail
   </th>
   <th style="width: 40%;">
    Certified Source
   </th>
  </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody>
  <tr>
   <td style="width: 60%; vertical-align: top;">
    The church originated in 1466 as the Oratory of the Crocetta, seat of the Company of Mary Queen of Paradise in the Pugliole district (Via Nuova di Reno), before merging in 1612–1613 with the Roman Confraternity of Saints Charles and Ambrose.
   </td>
   <td style="width: 40%; vertical-align: top;">
    Gian Nicolò Pasquali Alidosi (1633), Count Filippo Bianchi ("Thesoro delle Indulgenze", 1588), and 1638 Map of Bologna
   </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td style="width: 60%; vertical-align: top;">
    The current building results from reconstructions started in 1619 by architect Nicolò Barelli (consecrated 1644), continued by Giovanni Antonio Ambrosi in 1746 (vaults, apse, and small dome), and completed between 1855 and 1874 by Giulio Viscardi under the patronage of Marquis Giuseppe Da Via Bargellini.
   </td>
   <td style="width: 40%; vertical-align: top;">
    Giuseppe Guidicini ("Cose Notabili della Città di Bologna") and Historical Drawings from the Archive of the Church of San Carlo
   </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td style="width: 60%; vertical-align: top;">
    The facade bears the Latin inscription "IN HONOREM SANCTORUM CAROLI ET AMBROSII - REFECTUM MDCCCLXVI" and is topped by the clock built in 1865 by Antonio Franchini, featuring a quarter-hour bell cast by Brighenti in 1831.
   </td>
   <td style="width: 40%; vertical-align: top;">
    Monumental Epigraphy of Via San Carlo and District Historical Documentation
   </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td style="width: 60%; vertical-align: top;">
    The interior houses the High Altarpiece depicting "The Virgin of Paradise and Saints Charles and Ambrose" by Giovanni Battista Bolognini, as well as the statue of Saint Joseph sculpted by Angelo Piò in the chapel sponsored by Marquis Giuseppe Davia.
   </td>
   <td style="width: 40%; vertical-align: top;">
    Artworks Catalog of the Church of Saints Charles and Ambrose
   </td>
  </tr>
 </tbody>
</table>
</div>